{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing, add an upward level shift to the whole path (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR/t too low in the first half of the horizon).",
    "Turning-point lag: after an upside breakout (new 10–20 day high), the model reacts too slowly; front-load the adjustment (bigger correction in days 1–10 than days 20–30).",
    "Missed downside breaks: when a sharp sell-off begins (2–4 day drop), the model stays too high and forecasts a rebound that doesn’t materialize; apply a negative correction that increases with horizon (largest in days ~15–30).",
    "Over-optimism after peaks: following a local peak/spike, the model tends to project continued strength while outcomes mean-revert lower; cap post-peak forecasts and impose stronger mean reversion beyond ~10 trading days.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–7) bias is mostly ‘too low’ in rallies, but medium/long horizon (days 15–30) bias flips to ‘too high’ during drawdowns—use regime detection to switch the sign of correction by horizon.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth versus realized moves; widen expected range by increasing sensitivity to recent realized volatility (especially after regime shifts)."
  ]
}
